=== CURRENT WORK ===
(2026-08-05) Qt6+CEF viewer: testing in the VM.

Test VM rebuilt from scratch on kiosk-2026-08-04-amd64, now running under
libvirt as domain 'unitotem-test' with a fixed IP (192.168.122.50) instead of
port forwarding, so the manager keeps its real default ports 80/443.
Full procedure written down in VM-TESTING.md; helpers in tools/.

Verified this session, on the current code, with no manager restarts:
  - viewer starts, boot screen renders, CEF subprocesses healthy
  - hot-plug  -> Viewers/list pushed within ~1s, correct geometry
  - hot-unplug -> pushed within ~1s, back to 1 screen/1 window
  - window_id increments and is never reused (0 -> 1 -> 2)
  - systemctl stop returns in 66ms (the CEF/SIGTERM hang stays fixed)
  - no white flash observed on window creation after the black-background fix
    (sampled at ~3fps, which would miss a single-frame artefact)

FIXED this session - starting with zero screens no longer invents a screen.
QGuiApplication always keeps at least one QScreen, so with no output at all
the xcb backend leaves a placeholder named after the X display (':0.0', 0x0)
where a real output would be named after its RandR output. The manager was
treating it as a real screen: it opened a window on it and reported it.
_is_placeholder()/_real_screens() in manager/webview/app.py now filter it.
Verified: booting with every connector forced off reports screens=[]
windows=[], and plugging one in then opens exactly one window.

OPEN - unplugging the LAST screen goes unnoticed. Viewers/list keeps
reporting that screen and its window, and nothing is pushed. Root cause,
measured directly on the VM:
  - Qt emits NO signal whatsoever for this transition - not screenRemoved,
    not screenAdded, not geometryChanged/availableGeometryChanged/
    physicalSizeChanged/refreshRateChanged, not primaryScreenChanged.
  - In a bare QGuiApplication with no windows, Qt renames the QScreen in
    place to ':0.0' a few seconds later (geometry stays stale).
  - In the manager, which owns a window on that screen, not even the rename
    happens: it is still 'Virtual-1' 1280x800 more than 20s later.
So there is nothing in Qt's API to observe, and _is_placeholder() cannot
catch it either. Fixing it needs a RandR event source of our own. Options,
not yet decided (the first two are the realistic ones):
  a) python-xlib in a thread, selecting RRScreenChangeNotify/RROutputChange
     on the root window - clean and genuinely event-driven, but adds a
     dependency to requirements.txt and therefore a venv/.deb rebuild;
  b) QAbstractNativeEventFilter over xcb events - no new dependency, but the
     RandR event base has to be worked out to identify the events;
  c) udev/DRM monitoring (pyudev) - another dependency, further from the
     X-level truth Qt itself uses.
Until then this violates "state changes reach the web UI purely through
events" in CLAUDE.md. Note the manager itself stays perfectly healthy with
zero screens - it is only the reported state that goes stale.

FIXED this session - the built-in internal pages now render. They were
never visible before.
utils/models/assets.py:187-188 defines the 'no assets' and 'first boot'
placeholder assets as https://localhost/unitotem-no-assets and
.../unitotem-first-boot. The manager serves a self-signed certificate
(/etc/ssl/unitotem.pem), CEF validates it like any other site, and there is
no certificate-error handling anywhere in manager/ - so instead of the
welcome screen a device shows Chromium's grey broken-page error. Confirmed
in the VM: the CDP target list has an iframe on
https://localhost/unitotem-first-boot, the screen shows the error page, and
from inside the VM `curl` gets 307 with -k and fails outright without it.
This hits every real device on its very first boot, which is exactly when
the welcome/hotspot/QR screen is the only thing the user can see.
Fixed by pinning this node's own public key:
--ignore-certificate-errors-spki-list is set from the SPKI hash of
/etc/ssl/unitotem.pem (_own_cert_spki_hash in webview/app.py). Note that
RequestHandler.OnCertificateError, the obvious candidate, does NOT work
here and was tried first: the pages are loaded in an iframe by
boot-screen.html, and Chromium rejects certificate errors on subframes
without ever consulting the handler - the callback is simply never invoked.
A blanket --ignore-certificate-errors would have worked but would also stop
validating scheduled remote assets.

FIXED this session, found immediately behind that one - the first-boot page
required authentication. routers.scheduler ends in a catch-all
"/{path:path}" guarded by Depends(LOGMAN), and it was registered before
/unitotem-{page}, so the catch-all matched and the device's own screen
showed the login form. The route is now declared before the routers and is
served without a session but only to loopback (non-local callers get 404,
which also keeps the hotspot SSID and password off the network). Verified:
200 from inside the VM, 404 from the host, and the welcome screen with
hostname/IP/OS renders on the framebuffer.

MINOR - the welcome page shows a scrollbar on the right in the viewer.
Cosmetic, but it is a kiosk screen and nothing should ever scroll there.

FIXED this session - the image dominant-colour backdrop works again.
Three separate things were in the way, only one of them the obvious one:
  - the backend flattened "no colour chosen" into an explicit rgb(0,0,0)
    before sending it, so the viewer could not tell it apart from a
    deliberate black and never sampled the picture. It now sends null.
  - boot-screen.html is loaded over file://, so a scheduled image is
    cross-origin: drawing it into a canvas tainted it and getImageData threw
    SecurityError. CEF now runs with --disable-web-security and
    --allow-file-access-from-files, which is defensible here - this browser
    only ever shows what the operator scheduled, with no address bar, no user
    input and no cross-origin credentials to steal.
  - the client-side HEAD probe was refused for the same origin reason, which
    is why probe_media_type() lives in the backend.
Verified: a letterboxed image now has bars at srgb(159,113,106), its own
average colour, instead of black.

FIXED this session - viewer/playlist assignments now survive a restart, and
a disconnect no longer throws them away. They were only ever an in-memory
dict (serialize_assignments() had no caller at all), and unregister_webview
deleted the entry outright, so a viewer that merely dropped its connection
lost its playlist. Assignments are written beside the playlists and read
back at startup; a viewer picks its playlist up again when it registers.
Verified: schedule a URL, restart the manager, the page comes back on its
own.

PARTLY DONE - playback of real assets. Verified end to end: a web page and
an image both reach the screen through the scheduler, the image lands in the
image container with fit=contain applied. Still untested: video, audio,
cover/fill, explicit bg_color, default duration and per-asset volume.

=== NEXT ===
Decided, in order:
1. Updates page, as specified in CLAUDE.md: xterm.js console, daily
   background check, navbar hint. The apt/dpkg frontend rework goes with it -
   a design study exists (pty + APT::Status-Fd + debconf passthrough,
   dropping sudo entirely), and its stage 0 is a small bug fix, because the
   current non-interactive invocation does not merely default prompts, it
   fails hard on a conffile prompt and leaves the package unconfigured.
2. Last-screen detection via python-xlib in a thread, listening for RandR
   events (option a above). Adds a dependency, so it needs a venv rebuild in
   the VM and a .deb rebuild for real installs.
3. Compute the image backdrop colour in the backend (see the CORS entry).
4. The remaining playback tests (VM-TESTING.md section 7, item 7).

